From 0b6c9185362bace50d55d621a1e84cf56d786dcc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kayori <thatzokay@proton.me>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 09:42:39 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] grate: add a layout evaluator and packing diagnostics for
 fragment programs

Groundwork for a layout that follows NVIDIA's (the next patch). Nothing
here changes the program that is emitted; everything is off unless
GRATE_PACKING_DEBUG is set.

fp/layout.c is a symbolic evaluator. Every ALU slot and every unit
operation (interpolation, SFU, fetch) is an uninterpreted function of
the values it reads, and the result is a hash of what the output write
sees. Evaluated "sequentially" it models the steps as the emitter
writes them; evaluated with the hardware rules measured in
docs/grate-alu-latency.md (an ALU result is visible two packets later or
in a later round, a unit operation works on the row step % rows) it
models the chip. Two layouts of one program are equivalent when the
hashes are equal, which makes it the safety net for a scheduler. It
refuses what it does not model: spills, epochs, FX10 interpolation and
destinations above R15.

grate_program.c gets the diagnostics used to find those rules:
GRATE_PACKING_DEBUG=1 prints slot operations, plain copies and packets
per program and a dry run of a list scheduler over the ALU-only runs of
steps under different latency models, =2 prints the steps with their
unit operations and ALU slots (registers as allocated), and a layout
line per step; GRATE_LAYOUT_TRACE traces the evaluator. A replay hook
(GRATE_REPLAY, GRATE_REPLAY_N, GRATE_NUMROWS, GRATE_INDST) substitutes a
recorded program for the compiled one, for the ALU latency experiments
of docs/grate-alu-latency.md.

Checked on the tablet: the 25 self-checking tests of run-grate-tests.sh
give the same results with and without the patch.
